<section class="cc-casestudy"><div class="cc-casestudy__inner"><div class="cc-casestudy__img-col"><div class="cc-casestudy__img-wrap"><img src="/images/casper-cloaking-film-philadelphia-casestudy.webp" alt="Center City Philadelphia law firm glass conference room with Casper Cloaking Film installed on partitions" title="Casper Cloaking Film case study — Center City law firm" width="1264" height="848" loading="lazy" class="cc-casestudy__img" /><div class="cc-casestudy__badge"><span class="cc-casestudy__badge-label">Philadelphia Case Study</span></div></div><div class="cc-casestudy__stat-row"><div class="cc-casestudy__stat"><span class="cc-casestudy__stat-val">100%</span><span class="cc-casestudy__stat-label">screen content blacked out from corridors</span></div><div class="cc-casestudy__stat-div"></div><div class="cc-casestudy__stat"><span class="cc-casestudy__stat-val">Clear</span><span class="cc-casestudy__stat-label">views into conference rooms preserved</span></div><div class="cc-casestudy__stat-div"></div><div class="cc-casestudy__stat"><span class="cc-casestudy__stat-val">All</span><span class="cc-casestudy__stat-label">conference room glass treated</span></div></div></div><div class="cc-casestudy__content"><span class="pp-eyebrow">Case Study</span><h2 class="cc-casestudy__title">How a Broad Street law firm protected conference room screens without frosting the glass</h2><p class="cc-casestudy__lead">A Center City law firm with glass conference rooms along a high-traffic corridor needed to stop client materials from being visible to visitors, staff, and vendors walking past the lobby and elevator bank.</p><p class="cc-casestudy__body">The firm liked its open, collaborative office. Full frosted film would have solved visibility, but it would also have made the conference rooms feel closed off and visually heavy. Blinds were not a fit because they interrupted the design language and required staff to remember to close them before every sensitive meeting.</p><p class="cc-casestudy__body">Window Film Philadelphia installed Casper Cloaking Film on the interior surface of the conference room glass. After installation, LED and LCD display content appeared black from the hallway and lobby, while attorneys, clients, furniture, daylight, and the room architecture remained visible. The firm kept the transparent office aesthetic and removed the most obvious screen-data exposure.</p><blockquote class="cc-casestudy__quote"><p>&ldquo;Casper gave us the privacy outcome we needed without changing the character of the office. From the corridor, the rooms still feel open — the screens just stop broadcasting confidential work.&rdquo;</p><footer>Managing Partner &mdash; Center City law firm</footer></blockquote><a href="/contact/" class="cc-casestudy__link" aria-label="Schedule a Casper Cloaking Film assessment">Schedule a Casper assessment <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="13" height="13" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M18 13v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"/><line x1="10" y1="14" x2="21" y2="3"/></svg></a></div></div></section>

